INT. MUSEUM OF CLOCKS - GREAT HALL - NIGHT

A hundred timepieces, each telling a different hour. Grandfather cases line the walls like sentries. Pendulums swing out of rhythm, ticks overlapping in a thin, endless rain. At the center, under a glass dome, a gilded astronomical clock the size of a carriage glitters, its planets frozen mid-orbit.

Five minutes to midnight. The building is dark, sleeping.

In the shadow of a long-case clock, four figures crouch like cats.

SERAPHINA VOSS, 40s, in black from throat to ankle, headlamp dimmed to a red coin, leads. At her shoulder, BARTHOLOMEW QUILL, 60s, silk scarf, gloved fingers unscrewing the cover plate of a wall panel. KENJI AOKI, 20s, in a catering uniform with a pocketed screwdriver, carries a coil of fiber cable. DESPINA ARVANITIS, 30s, enormous eyes, a stethoscope hanging from her neck, taps a glass case with the delicacy of a safecracker.

A small radio crackles from Seraphina's belt.

LOOKOUT (V.O.)
(whispering)
Guard's on the east stairs. Two minutes.

SERAPHINA
(low)
Understood.

BARTHOLOMEW
Panel's open.

KENJI
Wires are color-coded.

BARTHOLOMEW
Ignore the colors. Follow the dust.

KENJI
Why?

BARTHOLOMEW
The ones that move are the ones they trust.

Kenji nods. Pinches a wire. Clips it.

DESPINA
Seraphina. The dome has a second lock.

SERAPHINA
Meaning?

DESPINA
Meaning it listens.

She presses the stethoscope to the glass. Closes her eyes. Her whole body bends toward the sound.

Tick, tick, tick, tick.

DESPINA (CONT'D)
(whispering)
It's counting. Three seconds to a click.

BARTHOLOMEW
Can you match it?

DESPINA
I can try.

She slides a slim tool beneath the dome's rim. Waits. A pendulum on the east wall swings out. Another answers.

Tick. Click. A pin falls.

LOOKOUT (V.O.)
One minute. He's at the landing.

SERAPHINA
Go.

Despina lifts the dome. Slow, trembling. It rises on invisible springs, a hush of glass. The planets of the clock gleam, close enough to touch.

Kenji slides a velvet bag under the gilded sun and eases it free. The gears whisper once. Then still.

BARTHOLOMEW
(hushed)
It's heavier than it looks.

KENJI
They always are.

LOOKOUT (V.O.)
Thirty seconds!

SERAPHINA
Seal it. Back to the wall.

They snap the dome back down. The pin settles. The clock gleams behind its glass, minus one small golden sun.

Somewhere in the hall, a bell tolls. Midnight. Every clock in the museum begins to strike, a hundred chimes, a hundred hours, clamor and swell.

The four crouch, hands over ears, as the hall rings around them like a cathedral. The guard's footsteps, if any, are drowned entirely.

KENJI
(laughing, shouting)
That's the best alarm I ever heard!

SERAPHINA
Quiet!

The last chime fades. Silence, then a single tick, then a hundred.

LOOKOUT (V.O.)
He's gone to the west wing. You're clear.

Bartholomew exhales. Despina leans on the long-case clock, trembling and grinning. Kenji laughs without sound. Seraphina lets go of the radio.

SERAPHINA
Walk. Don't run. Let's go home.
